1790606491.520	The Early Days of Printing
1790606491.520	
1790606491.520	The concept of printing dates back to ancient China, where paper was first invented in the 2nd century AD. The first known printed book was the "Bamboo Book," a collection of Buddhist scriptures that was printed on paper in the 8th century. However, it was not until the 10th century that the first movable-type printing press was developed in China. The press, known as the "Rituo Press," was invented by the Chinese monk Wuzheng, who created a machine that could print individual characters on a sheet of paper.
1790606491.520	
1790606491.520	In the 12th century, the printing press was introduced to Europe by the monks of the Benedictine monastery in St. Gall, Switzerland. The monks, who were familiar with the Chinese printing press, adapted the technology to create a machine that could print individual characters on a sheet of paper. This early printing press was called the "Monumentum Press," and it was used to print the first printed books in Europe.
1790606491.520	
